---
import LegacyLayout from '@/layouts/LegacyLayout.astro';
import FounderCard from '@/components/legacy/FounderCard.astro';
import FoundationalReading from '@/components/legacy/FoundationalReading.astro';
import TestimonyPullQuote from '@/components/legacy/TestimonyPullQuote.astro';
import { FOUNDERS } from '@/data/founders';

const founders = FOUNDERS.filter((f) => f.category === 'founder');
const originWitnesses = FOUNDERS.filter((f) => f.category === 'origin-witness');
const cherie = originWitnesses.find((f) => f.slug === 'cherie-karo-schwartz');
const laurie = originWitnesses.find((f) => f.slug === 'laurie-obrien');
const faculty = FOUNDERS.filter((f) => f.category === 'faculty');
const contributors = FOUNDERS.filter((f) => f.category === 'contributor');
---

<LegacyLayout
  title="Founders"
  subPage="founders"
  eyebrow="The people who built the Legacy &mdash; and the institution that resourced them"
  description="The four founders, the origin witnesses, the contributing faculty, and the critical early contributors who shaped Developmental Theatre."
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-14">

    <section>
      <p class="text-ink-700 text-base">
        Developmental Theatre cohered in the 1970s around four founders and the
        University of Colorado &mdash; the institution that resourced them. What
        began as a single touring company grew into an M.A. programme, an
        international network of successor theatres, and a fifty-year practice
        still active today.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-ink-900 text-2xl">The four founders</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-2">
        Richard Knaub, Chuck Wilcox, Lola Wilcox, and Martin Cobin launched the
        Colorado Caravan at CU Boulder in 1971. Each brought a distinct
        discipline &mdash; direction, playwriting, education, scholarship
        &mdash; and each stayed with the practice long enough to hand it on.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid list-none gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        {founders.map((f) => (
          <li>
            <FounderCard founder={f} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-ink-900 text-2xl">Theatre Games origins</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-2">
        Two voices from the Caravan floor carry the origin practice forward in
        their own words:
      </p>

      {/* CLIENT REVIEW: first-person testimony drafted from vision spec §3 Doc #3
          fragments (the Cherie compilation → Theatre Games section; the Laurie
          Workshop Manual → both sections). Verbatim source lives in client
          Drive; awaits reconciliation. */}
      {cherie && (
        <TestimonyPullQuote speaker={cherie.name} role={cherie.years} year="1976&ndash;7">
          I was in the 1976&ndash;7 Colorado Caravan troupe. What we made together
          &mdash; the games, the warm-ups, the shared vocabulary of ensemble
          &mdash; I later gathered into a compilation that has become the base
          for the <a href="/theatre-games/" class="hover:text-teal-600">Theatre
          Games section</a> of this website. Every game there has a lineage back
          to the Caravan floor.
        </TestimonyPullQuote>
      )}
      {laurie && (
        <TestimonyPullQuote speaker={laurie.name} role="Facilitator, Workshop Manual author">
          The workshops we ran after every performance became a discipline of
          their own. I&rsquo;ve been writing the practice down as the{' '}
          <a href="/legacy/essays/workshop-manual/" class="hover:text-teal-600">
            Workshop Manual
          </a>{' '}
          for facilitators; when it publishes it will live in both this Legacy
          section and the Theatre Games section.
        </TestimonyPullQuote>
      )}
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-ink-900 text-2xl">Contributing faculty</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-2">
        CU faculty whose scholarship and languages widened the Caravan&rsquo;s
        reach beyond the departmental theatre programme.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid list-none gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        {faculty.map((f) => (
          <li>
            <FounderCard founder={f} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-ink-900 text-2xl">Institutional support</h2>

      {/* CLIENT REVIEW: prose drafted from vision spec §4.2 (sacred numbers) and
          §3 Doc #3 (CU-contributions and Chautauqua-Park passages). Awaits
          reconciliation with the source passage in client Drive. */}
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-2">
        The University of Colorado was more than an academic host &mdash; it was
        the material ground the Caravan grew from. CU supplied the van the
        company drove, the gas that filled it, the rehearsal space where
        scripts were tested, and even Lola&rsquo;s desk. Housing came from the
        Chautauqua Park cabins, whose shared kitchens and mountain-porch
        dinners built the offstage rapport that made the ensemble work.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4">
        Federal Title III funding underwrote the touring. When the National
        Endowment for the Arts added a Dissemination &amp; Diffusion Grant, the
        Caravan&rsquo;s reach multiplied. At its height the company was
        performing for over <strong>30,000 children</strong> each year, and for
        <strong>100,000 or more</strong> when All-Town performances combined
        elementary, middle-school, and high-school audiences under a single
        roof. A national evaluation team designated the programme
        &ldquo;exemplary.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4">
        The same funding stream built an M.A. programme in Developmental
        Theatre at CU, granted within three years and led by Dr. Martin Cobin.
        The programme&rsquo;s graduates seeded companies across the country and
        beyond &mdash; the Overland Stage Company in Wyoming in 1978, followed
        by successor theatres in Maine, Montana, Hawaii, Denmark, and
        Australia. Across fifty years the Caravan and its offshoots have
        performed for over <strong>six million people on three
        continents</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-ink-900 text-2xl">Foundational reading</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-2">
        The theoretical and practical texts the Caravan&rsquo;s founders drew
        on as they built the practice. The list is short by design &mdash;
        these six works anchor most of Developmental Theatre&rsquo;s
        vocabulary.
      </p>
      <FoundationalReading />
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-ink-900 text-2xl">Critical early contributors</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-2">
        People whose sustained work with the Caravan, the CSF outreach
        programme, or the successor theatres kept the practice alive across
        decades. Not founders; not faculty; indispensable.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-6 grid list-none gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        {contributors.map((f) => (
          <li>
            <FounderCard founder={f} />
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-ink-900 text-2xl">
        Continuity through the Colorado Shakespeare Festival
      </h2>

      {/* CLIENT REVIEW: CSF outreach continuity prose drafted from vision spec §3
          Doc #3 (Devin era → Chuck-as-Will → Giguere). Awaits source reconciliation. */}
      {/* TODO(client): CSF Facebook video URL for outreach programme &mdash;
          link activates once URL arrives. */}
      {/* TODO(client): Will Power article PDF hosting &mdash; permission
          pending; when granted, host under /public/legacy/ and swap the
          in-text reference for a hyperlink. */}
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-2">
        The Caravan&rsquo;s Shakespeare influence carried into the Colorado
        Shakespeare Festival&rsquo;s outreach programme. Richard Devin&rsquo;s
        era established outreach as central to CSF&rsquo;s mission; Chuck
        Wilcox toured for CSF as Will Shakespeare himself, taking the character
        into schools across the state.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4">
        The programme continues today under Amanda Giguere. CSF publishes a
        Facebook video on the outreach programme (link pending) and a
        Will Power article (hosting pending) that document the work in the
        current era.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-display text-ink-900 text-2xl">What came of it</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-2">
        The Caravan and the M.A. programme ceased when their institutional
        sponsors moved on. What they built, however, did not. Decades of
        exploration, teaching, touring, and revision have produced
        <em>Developmental Theatre: Fearless Creativity</em> &mdash; the current
        continuation of the work these founders began.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-label="Related pages" class="border-ivory-200 border-t pt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display text-ink-900 text-lg">Continue</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 list-none space-y-2">
        <li>
          <a href="/legacy/timeline/" class="hover:text-teal-600">
            Explore the Grand Timeline &rarr;
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/legacy/honoring-our-guides/" class="hover:text-teal-600">
            See Honoring Our Guides &rarr;
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/legacy/research/" class="hover:text-teal-600">
            Read the Research abstract &rarr;
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

  </div>
</LegacyLayout>
